WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
The first film showed a wet lining pulling each alveolus smaller, and surfactant

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.320
easing that pull. Now breathe in fully and blow out hard.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.820
What does that blast reveal?

4
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.433
Every breath has two pressure drops.

5
00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:22.973
Across the lung's wall, transpulmonary pressure matches the lung's recoil.

6
00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:27.053
Along the airways, a second drop moves the air.

7
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:29.813
Pleural pressure pays for both.

8
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:35.767
Blow out hard, and the expiratory muscles push pleural pressure above atmospheric.

9
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:41.727
Alveolar pressure is then recoil plus pleural pressure, and air rushes out.

10
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.270
But the airways sit in the same pleural space.

11
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.490
Friction uses up pressure along them.

12
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.930
Where the pressure inside falls to pleural pressure, the airway beyond is squeezed.

13
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.880
This is dynamic compression.

14
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.820
A weak recoil, or narrowed airways upstream of the squeeze point, means a low

15
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:03.820
maximal flow.

16
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.033
A spirometer records the blast.

17
00:01:08.653 --> 00:01:11.333
FEV1 is the volume out in the first second.

18
00:01:12.573 --> 00:01:18.713
FVC is the whole. In a made-up volunteer they are 1.5 and 3.0 litres.

19
00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:23.293
The ratio, 0.50, is below a made-up limit.

20
00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:25.793
Obstructive.

21
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:27.933
Here is the one idea.

22
00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:33.593
Pleural pressure pays for the lung's recoil and for moving air through the airways.

23
00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:37.253
Disease changes the recoil, or the resistance.

24
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:44.467
Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, so recoil is too little, and maximal flow is low.

25
00:01:45.807 --> 00:01:49.387
Without walls tugging them open, small airways compress earlier.

26
00:01:50.807 --> 00:01:54.307
FEV1 falls more than FVC, and the ratio falls.

27
00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:02.270
Now fibrosis. Scarred tissue gives the lung too much recoil, and total lung capacity

28
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:07.450
falls. Does its ratio of FEV1 to FVC fall too?

29
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:20.037
No. High recoil drives high flow for the volume, and stiff tissue holds the airways

30
00:02:20.037 --> 00:02:24.157
open. FEV1 and FVC fall together.

31
00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:26.977
The ratio is normal or high.

32
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.980
That is the restrictive pattern: a low total lung capacity.

33
00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.480
A spirometer cannot see gas that never leaves the lung.

34
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.280
One made-up volunteer had a normal ratio.

35
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.520
A body box found the low capacity.

36
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.740
A newborn without enough surfactant has respiratory distress syndrome: too much

37
00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:55.320
surface recoil. Each breath needs a large negative swing in pleural pressure.

38
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.260
That pulls the soft chest wall inward, and at low volume the alveoli close.

39
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:05.403
So read a sick lung by what each breath pays for.

40
00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:10.563
Emphysema, fibrosis and a surfactant-poor lung change the recoil.

41
00:03:12.163 --> 00:03:14.663
Asthma and chronic bronchitis narrow the airways.
